before:
```typescript
class Basket {
  label: string;
  constructor(label: string) {
    this.label = label;
  }
}
const first = new Basket("Fruit");
const second = new Basket("Bread");
console.log(first.label);
console.log(second.label);
```
This prints Fruit, then Bread. new Basket("Fruit") passes text to constructor(label:string) and produces a Basket instance. this.label = label stores the input on that instance. The field declaration label:string describes its type; it does not choose a value. Each new call creates a different object. console.log reads each instance's label.

```typescript
class Basket {
  label: string;
  constructor(label: string) {
    this.label = label;
  }
}
const first = new Basket("Fruit");
const second = new Basket("Bread");
first.label = "Vegetables";
console.log(first.label);
console.log(second.label);
```
This prints Vegetables, then Bread. Trace the changed value through the same rule.

